6 minutes Posted by Lyubov Dobretsova 1192
Patients who are aware of the normal blood glucose level will panic and wonder if they have diabetes when their test results show 7 mmol/L or higher. Such a result is certainly concerning and requires further diagnostic testing.
However, doctors warn that a blood sugar level of 7 mmol/L or higher does not always indicate the development of a dangerous disease. This reaction can be caused by minor malfunctions in the internal organs and systems, as well as by the negative effects of external factors. To prevent the development of hyperglycemia, it is necessary to identify the cause of the deviation and eliminate it.
Normal blood sugar levels for people of different ages
Before you wonder what a blood sugar test result of 7 to 7.9 mmol/L means, you need to understand what indicators are considered normal in international medicine. There is no single normal blood sugar value for adults and children, as the concentration of this substance depends on age.
Generally, in healthy men and women, blood sugar taken in the morning on an empty stomach should not exceed 5.5 mmol/L. The lower limit is 3.3 mmol/L. If there is no pathological process in most people, the test will show a result of 4.5 to 4.7 units.
The only case where a healthy person has elevated blood sugar is immediately after a meal. This reaction is characteristic of both adults and young children. In patients aged 60 to 90 years, the normal range of indicators is slightly different and ranges from 4.6 to 6.4 mmol/L.
If a blood test shows a result of 6.4 units, this is an occasion to think about your health and undergo further diagnostic testing, as a similar result may be a sign of the development of diabetes mellitus. Therefore, if blood sugar on an empty stomach is 7 mmol/L or higher, it is a cause for concern.
What does a blood sugar level of 7 mean?
During a meal, the body is saturated with carbohydrates. If the diet is based on simple carbohydrates, which consist of the least structural elements, the glucose level will rise very quickly. Glucose enters the bloodstream through the pancreas. This organ produces insulin, which compensates for the sugar level.
If blood sugar reaches a value of 7 units (7.1, 7.2, 7.3, and higher), this means that the properties of cell membranes are impaired and they are starving. In this case, the doctor will prescribe another test for the patient, which will help to confirm or refute the presumptive diagnosis.
Often, it turns out that hyperglycemia is a temporary phenomenon caused by the negative effects of external factors. If the test is repeated and shows a reliable result, the patient should carefully prepare for it and follow all the doctor's recommendations. The most important condition is to abstain from food for 10-12 hours before giving the biomaterial.
Only a glass of water is allowed in the morning. It is also worth avoiding emotional experiences and increased physical exertion the day before, as they can also cause a false positive result. If the patient strictly follows all the recommendations, but the analysis shows an increased glucose level, for example, 7.4 or 7.8 mmol/L, this indicates the beginning of a pathological process and requires additional tests and diagnostics.
It should be noted that diabetes is almost never asymptomatic. The symptoms of the disease can be felt even in the early stages. Most patients complain of thirst, frequent dizziness, itching on the skin, and the appearance of pustules, a weakened immune system, and impaired vision.
This is why a false positive result can occur.
If the second test shows that blood sugar does not exceed the norm, then there is no reason to worry. As practice shows, a blood sugar test often shows a false positive result.
The reasons for a temporary increase in this component may be:
- increased physical activity the night before;
- excessive work and lack of sleep;
- stress, emotional shock;
- prolonged use of certain medications (hormonal drugs, oral contraceptives, diuretics);
- overeating;
- inflammatory process in the pancreas;
- childbirth;
- endocrine disorders in the body;
- recent surgery.
If the patient is constantly taking medications, it is important to inform the doctor who interprets the result.
What to do when the sugar level is above 7
If the test shows that the glucose concentration exceeds 7 mmol/L, this reaction indicates the development of diabetes in the patient. Prediabetic condition is diagnosed only if the indicator is in the range of 6.5 to 7 mmol/L.
However, although these diagnoses differ significantly, the initial treatment will be practically the same. The doctor will tell the patient what to do and how to reduce the concentration of this component. The main condition is to correct the patient's lifestyle.
If measures are not taken in time, the glucose concentration will gradually increase, which will negatively affect the condition of the internal organs and systems. This increases the likelihood of irreversible consequences for the patient.
If blood sugar is 7.5, 7.6, 7.7 mmol/L or higher, the following measures will help to restore the normal level of this component:
- give up bad habits, including smoking;
- adjust the diet. The basis of the diet should be foods that contain a minimum amount of carbohydrates;
- if the patient is overweight, they need to lose weight. Therefore, the diet should not only be low in calories but also low in carbohydrates;
- the patient should lead an active lifestyle, as moderate physical activity helps to improve the condition.
To prevent diabetes mellitus from progressing to a complicated form that requires constant insulin injections, it is necessary to learn how to control the disease. The latest drug, Insumed, helps to get rid of diabetes at the cellular level: the drug restores the production of insulin (which is impaired in type 2 diabetes) and regulates the synthesis of this hormone (which is important in type 1 diabetes).
Detailed information about this drug is available at. To buy Insumed at a discount, go to the official website.
Diet correction
The basis of treatment for diabetes in adults and children is diet correction. If you do not eat foods with a high content of carbohydrates and exclude harmful foods, you can not only normalize the glucose concentration in the blood but also maintain it at the required level.
First, the patient should exclude products with easily digestible carbohydrates. It is also recommended to reduce the consumption of products containing starch. Another condition is adherence to fractional nutrition. You need to eat 5-6 times a day, but the portions should be small.
It is recommended to exclude the following foods and drinks from the diet:
- granulated sugar, starch;
- strong coffee and strong tea;
- pastries and baked goods;
- potatoes (especially fried), fatty meat and fish;
- alcoholic beverages;
- soda;
- sweets (honey, chocolate, candy, jam).
The diet should be dominated by foods with a high content of dietary fiber (which reduces the properties of starch and sugar), fresh vegetables, and dairy products with a minimum fat content.
Low-fat varieties of meat and fish, as well as cereals, are allowed, but they should be present in limited quantities. Such a diet will not only prevent an increase in glucose concentration but also help to lose weight.
Conclusion
Diabetes mellitus is a disease that negatively affects the entire future life of the patient. Therefore, it is much wiser to try to prevent its occurrence. To do this, you need to not only follow preventive measures but also take a blood sugar test every 6 months (even if there are no indications).
If the test shows that the concentration of this component exceeds the norm, the doctor will tell you whether this is dangerous and what measures need to be taken to return the indicator to a normal level.

