What is a Neanderthal:
A Neanderthal (Homo neanderthalensis) is an extinct human species that was part of the evolutionary process of modern humans.
The first Neanderthals appeared approximately 350,000 years ago on the present-day European and Asian continents. However, they mysteriously disappeared approximately 30,000 years ago.
This human species is named after the Neander Valley in Germany, where the first fossils of the species were discovered.
There is still no consensus among researchers and scientists about what caused the extinction of the Neanderthals. There are several theories, but the most accepted point is that drastic climate change caused their gradual disappearance.
Some researchers believe that Neanderthals were a subspecies of Homo sapiens (Homo sapiens neanderthalensis), which coexisted with modern humans (Homo sapiens sapiens) for approximately 10,000 years.
Contrary to popular belief, modern studies show that Neanderthals may have had intellectual abilities equal to those of Homo sapiens sapiens.
The fact is that Neanderthals share more than 99% of their DNA with humans today. Such evidence proves that Homo sapiens sapiens and Neanderthals interbred and produced hybrid species. However, there are still significant morphological differences that distinguish Neanderthals from modern humans.
