Carbon monoxide is a gas present in everyday life and can be very harmful.
Image by Gilles Tarabiscuité from Pixabay.
When we hear about carbon monoxide, represented by the molecular formula CO, we quickly associate the gas with danger, pollution, or poisoning. But let's try to understand better what carbon monoxide is. Carbon monoxide is a colorless, odorless, and tasteless gas that is flammable and dangerous (because it is a chemical asphyxiant that can cause poisoning). Learn about its main sources of emission and avoid the risk of poisoning.

Sources that emit carbon monoxide
Carbon monoxide is released into the environment from natural or artificial (human-caused) sources. Natural sources of emission can include volcanic activity, electrical discharges, and emissions of natural gas. However, anthropogenic sources of emissions account for approximately 60% of the carbon monoxide present in the troposphere. All of this is a product of incomplete combustion, that is, the burning of wood, coal, and minerals; gasoline; kerosene; diesel oil, or gas when there is not enough oxygen to consume all of these fuels.
Carbon monoxide can also originate from the photochemical oxidation of volatile organic compounds (VOCs) in the atmosphere or on the surface of water bodies. In the atmosphere, the compound can oxidize and form carbon dioxide; in surface waters, which are saturated with it, microorganisms can use this compound as an energy source.
The most common sources of carbon monoxide emissions, releasing the highest concentrations of the gas into the atmosphere (millions of tons), are fires that occur in forests around the world, and the gas emitted from vehicle exhaust.
Use
Carbon monoxide is often used in industry as a reducing agent, removing oxygen from some compounds, which occurs in the production of iron and other metals, and in the synthesis of various organic substances, such as acetic acid, formic acid, plastics, methanol, and others. In World War II, it was used in gas chambers in concentration camps.
- Iron: importance and impacts of its extraction
Carbon monoxide poisoning
According to some studies, the main way of exposure to carbon monoxide is through inhalation. Acute poisoning can be fatal due to the gas's affinity for hemoglobin in red blood cells, which carries oxygen (O2) to the tissues of all organs in the body. The affinity of hemoglobin for carbon monoxide is up to 240 times greater than for O2.
After inhalation, the gas is quickly absorbed into the lungs, crosses the alveolar, capillary, and placental membranes, and binds stably to hemoglobin in the bloodstream. Poisoning occurs in humans when carbon monoxide competes with oxygen for hemoglobin, reducing the release of oxygen bound under hemoglobin, and thus preventing transport and reducing the amount of oxygen available in the tissues, causing death by asphyxiation.
Effects
The existence of chronic carbon monoxide poisoning due to prolonged exposure to low concentrations can cause cumulative toxic effects, such as insomnia, headache, fatigue, reduced physical capacity, learning, and work, dizziness, vertigo, nausea, vomiting, visual disturbances, hearing disturbances, respiratory diseases, anorexia, Parkinson's disease, cardiac ischemia, heart disease, and atherosclerosis. In the elderly, it causes an increase in mortality from acute myocardial infarction.
Symptoms of mild carbon monoxide poisoning include fainting, a feeling of confusion, headache, dizziness, and other flu-like symptoms.
Prolonged exposure can lead to severe poisoning in the central nervous system, in the heart, and even to death. The consequences of acute poisoning are almost always permanent.
- Insomnia: what it is, teas, medications, causes, and how to end insomnia
Air quality
National air quality standards were signed in 1976 by the Brazilian Environmental Institute (Ibama) and approved by the National Environment Council (Conama). In April 2013, Decree No. 51113 was published, containing stricter air quality parameters. In the case of carbon monoxide, the national standard reaches 9 ppm for an 8-hour sampling time. As for the air quality index adopted by Cetesb - Environmental Sanitation Technology Company, the qualification of CO in the air for 8 hours of sampling is:
- Good quality: 9 ppm,
- Moderate quality: 9 to 11 ppm;
- Poor quality: 11 to 13 ppm;
- Very poor quality: 13 to 15 ppm;
- Bad quality: more than 15 ppm.
- What is air pollution? Learn about the causes and types
It is important to pay attention to this air quality index, especially in winter and if we have children, the elderly, or people with heart problems at home, as high levels of carbon monoxide in the air can be more harmful to these groups of people.
How to prevent poisoning
High levels of carbon monoxide in the air can be harmful to our health, and we must control the sources of the gas that we have in our homes, as they also have the potential for poisoning, such as gas or petroleum-fueled heaters, stoves, wood-burning stoves, gas stoves, fireplaces, and car exhaust. We can avoid these sources of poisoning with a few suggestions:
- Make sure that all equipment in your home is properly installed and working;
- Make sure that the furnace, chimneys, and pipes are inspected and cleaned every year;
- If you are using a fireplace, make sure that the pipes and chimney are open;
- Do not heat houses with gas equipment;
- Make sure that the furnace and stove are vented to the outside and that there are no leaks in the exhaust systems;
- Do not burn coal in any enclosed space;
- Do not leave gas-powered tools or vehicles in the garage, workshop, or other enclosed space;
- Never use gas heaters for showers in bathrooms without ventilation;
- Use range hoods when cooking - understand why in the article "Danger lives at home: substances released during cooking can harm your health");
- Place plants that clean the air in your home or workplace;
- Improve air quality with a little daily care - see in the article "Mistakes and successes in the house";
- In large cities, do not exercise during peak hours.

