Totalitarianism
What is totalitarianism:
Totalitarianism or a totalitarian regime is a political system based on an ideology that establishes the leader of the nation as the absolute ruler, controlling the rights of citizens for the supposed general benefit of the state. The totalitarian leader can be an individual, a group, or a party.
A totalitarian government exercises complete control over the media and generally eliminates private schools, resulting in public schools teaching according to the party's guidelines.
Freedom of religion also does not exist in a totalitarian state, as it only allows the existence of churches whose ministers cooperate with the government. Free trade unions are also illegal.
Political Totalitarianism
In totalitarianism, only one political party can exist, led by an absolute leader. This dictator maintains power by using force, violence, and psychological and physical torture against individuals who do not respect government laws.
The dominant political party determines the economic guidelines that the state must follow.
The Origin of Totalitarianism
The definition of a totalitarian regime emerged in the early twentieth century, after the end of the First World War. During this period, countries suffered the negative consequences of the war years.
Mass unemployment and increasing poverty were some of the main factors that led to changes in the social and political structures of the time.
The world crisis of 1929, which began in the United States, was the culmination of this stagnation, representing the first major failure of the capitalist system and liberalism.
Measures were created to revive the economy and strengthen capitalism, such as state intervention in economic affairs. However, in Europe, several party leaders went further and exploited the "social despair" to impose political ideologies based on a strong sense of nationalism. Thus, one of the strongest examples of totalitarianism emerged: German Nazism.
However, the first modern totalitarian state to be created was not Nazi Germany (1933-1945) or Fascist Italy (1925-1943), but Soviet Stalinism. This totalitarian government emerged from the communist revolution in Russia in 1919, even during the First World War.
The Difference Between Authoritarianism and Totalitarianism
Authoritarianism, unlike totalitarianism, which presents itself as an extremist political experience, consists of an emphasis on state power over a republic or union.
Authoritarian rule, like a totalitarian regime, abuses power to control the state as much as possible. However, this control is in the hands of a group of legislators and does not focus solely on one leading personality, as in totalitarianism.
Another important difference between the two concepts is the presence of a strong ideological sense in totalitarianism, which acts as the guiding principle of the entire system.
Right-Wing and Left-Wing Totalitarianism
Totalitarian regimes of the right and left have similar characteristics in their structure, but differ in their different political ideologies.
Direct Totalitarianism
Nazism and Fascism are two of the main examples of right-wing totalitarian governments, mainly for:
- supporting the bourgeoisie;
- preserving the traditional ideological values of the state (religion, family, nationality, etc.);
- having strong support from the clergy;
- keeping trade union organizations under strict state control or, in many cases, banning/suppressing these groups.
Left-Wing Totalitarianism
Stalinism can be considered an excellent example of what left-wing totalitarianism would be, mainly by:
- promoting the end of private property;
- mandatory collective agricultural and commercial production;
- eliminating religion in the political environment;
- based on the ideas of socialism.
Apart from these specificities, both use other characteristics that define a totalitarian government: censorship of the media, militarization, nationalism, the existence of a single party, and so on.
Find out what Stalinism is.
Totalitarianism in Brazil
Brazil was also subjected to a totalitarian regime in the mid-twentieth century, during the government of Getúlio Vargas. This period became known as Estado Novo, characterized by the closure of the National Congress and the complete concentration of power in the hands of the president, who had remnants of Italian Fascism.
Brazilian totalitarianism under the government of Vargas ended with the beginning of the Second World War, when Brazil declared opposition to totalitarian regimes in Europe. Thus, an ideological inconsistency arose in the government's actions.
See also the meaning of dictatorship.
Characteristics of Totalitarianism
For a nation to be considered totalitarian, its government must adhere to some of these characteristics:
- Complete concentration of power in the hands of a single ruler (person or group);
- Anti-democratic (no room for democracy);
- Citizens do not have individual rights;
- Citizens have minimal participation in decision-making in the state;
- The leader (dictator) decides according to his will, whether political or economic;
- When there is a legislative and judicial power, it is completely subordinate to the ruler;
- Militarism (excessive use of military force and military equipment to control the population and protect the state);
- Nationalism (constant elevation of love for the nation and praise of the leader's figure);
- Mandate for an indefinite period (no renewal of power);
- Expansionist (desire to conquer other territories, especially in war);
- Mass government propaganda;
- Control of the population based on terror and fear (reactionary attitude towards opponents, practices of torture and murder);
- Existence of a single party.
See also the meaning of Fascism and Nazism.

