Lethargic encephalitis – causative agent and diagnosis, symptoms, and treatment

Contents
A mysterious disease that was epidemic at the beginning of the last century, characterized by sleep disturbances, confusion, and motor disorders, is called lethargic encephalitis. This article will help you learn about the types, symptoms, pathogens, and ways to treat encephalitis, as well as learn about methods for diagnosing and preventing the disease.

What is lethargic encephalitis?
The first study of lethargic disease, also known as "sleeping sickness," was conducted in 1915 by Austrian neuropathologist K. Economo. Lethargic encephalitis is a viral infection that causes inflammation of brain cells. The main area of damage in the Economo virus epidemic is usually the basal ganglia and the brainstem. In our time, lethargic disease is rare, but at the dawn of the twentieth century, epidemic encephalitis swept through many countries, most often leading to the death of patients.

Causative agent of encephalitis
Doctors can only guess what might be the pathogen for encephalitis, as its nature has not yet been discovered. It is assumed that encephalitis is caused by the Economo virus, which was originally contained in human body fluids – saliva and mucus. The supposed virus cannot exist separately from the human body. Scientists believe that the main purpose of the pathogen is to attack the central nervous system, where it enters through the respiratory tract. The activity of the virus causes the appearance of the first clinical signs of the disease.

Symptoms of encephalitis
Lethargic disease occurs in two phases – acute and chronic, with a time span ranging from one month to several years. The classic appearance of Economo's lethargic disease in the initial phase is characterized by a significant increase in body temperature – up to 40°C. The first manifestations of lethargic disease are similar to the symptoms of a cold. In addition, the following primary symptoms of encephalitis are distinguished:
- headaches and muscle pain;
- nausea, vomiting;
- feeling of weakness, fatigue;
- depressive, apathetic state;
- sore throat;
- constant drowsiness (hypersomnia);
- sleep disturbances;
- catarrhal phenomena in the respiratory tract.

Acute encephalitis
The first external signs of encephalitis in humans gradually develop into the acute form of lethargic disease, which can last up to a month. The following symptoms can indicate acute encephalitis:
- There is hypersensitivity, which is unbearable – the patient falls asleep in any position, dreams can last up to three weeks, and they enter a lethargic state.
- The daily rhythm is disturbed – a person can sleep during the day and at night.
- The muscles of the oculomotor nerves are poorly functioning – expansion of the pupils, visual disturbances, paralysis of the eyeballs.
- There are neurological disorders – dizziness, vestibular ataxia (impaired coordination of movements, gait), autonomic dysfunction.
- Muscle spasms appear – twitching, prolonged convulsions, tonic body movements.

Chronic encephalitis
The transition from the acute period ends with chronic encephalitis. The symptoms of the febrile phase persist, replaced by the following manifestations of lethargic disease:
- persistent migraine;
- depression;
- tearfulness, mood swings;
- pleocytosis;
- rapid fatigue;
- Insomnia or drowsiness;
- aggressiveness, cruelty;
- increased sweating;
- hyperkinesia;
- excessive salivation;
- cerebellar syndrome;
- changes in appetite, leading to exhaustion or obesity;
- decreased intelligence;
- lethargic state;
- dysfunction of the sebaceous glands.
All patients suffer from Parkinson's syndrome, which causes degenerative changes in the psyche – slow movements, indiscriminate speech, mental retardation, tremors in the hands. There are violations of cardiac and respiratory activity, eye spasms (occlusive crisis), dysfunction of the endocrine system. Some patients are diagnosed with hallucinations. Severe cases of the disease are accompanied by a coma and can lead to a rapid fatal outcome.

Causes of lethargic encephalitis
Scientists cannot determine the causes of lethargic encephalitis. It is assumed that the encephalitis virus is found in the saliva and mucus of humans. Infection occurs through airborne droplets. For example, if an infected person sneezes or coughs on a healthy person, the virus quickly enters the brain tissue through the respiratory tract, where it accumulates and re-enters the bloodstream. The incubation period of the Economo virus can range from a few days to several years. Another possible cause of infection is a sick pregnant mother to the future child.

According to statistical data, the disease progresses more often in the cold season, but there are cases of infection in the warm season. Men and women are infected with the virus equally often, possibly between the ages of 20 and 40. Residents of cities suffered more often than those in rural areas. Outbreaks of the virus were observed in small groups, where people communicated daily.

Diagnosis of Economo encephalitis
In the acute phase of the disease, doctors rarely make a correct diagnosis, as its initial signs are similar to ordinary colds. The disease is easier to detect in the chronic phase, when specific symptoms are clearly expressed. In our time, such a virus is extremely rare, which makes its definition difficult.

When making a diagnosis, more attention should be paid to sleep disturbances, mental disorders, and visual dysfunction. In combination with secondary symptoms – elevated temperature and the presence of an unspecified infection in the body – this will determine the picture of the disease. In recent years, the diagnosis of Economo encephalitis has been simplified by MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) of the brain.

Treatment of lethargic encephalitis
Lethargic disease is difficult to treat in any phase. In the chronic form, which is accompanied by Parkinson's syndrome, the neuropathologist prescribes trighexyphenidyl, levodopa. Tropacin, cyclodol are often prescribed. Secondary mental disorders are treated with psychotropic drugs, which are prescribed by a doctor after a thorough examination of the patient. Physiotherapy exercises – warm baths, special physical exercises that provide adequate physical activity.

Currently, there are no specific therapies or special vaccines. All measures are aimed at alleviating symptoms and relieving the patient's pain. During an acute episode, encephalitis is treated in the following ways:
- antiviral drugs;
- dehydrating drugs;
- desensitizers;
- pyridoxine, combined with ascorbic acid, cyanocobalamin;
- belladonna solution;
- mixed with spring water.

Prevention of lethargic encephalitis
There is no effective prevention of lethargic encephalitis, because the pathogens of the disease, the Economo virus, caused a high mortality rate in the last century, and although the disease is rare in the modern world, an epidemic can erupt at any time. The best prevention is to increase immunity with proper nutrition, the use of vitamins, and the abandonment of bad habits. Close contact with other people should be cautious, as accidental infection is possible, but the prospects for the disease are very disappointing.

